For a new checkout, run:
./bootstrap.sh
It configures libEtPan with the options required by the Xcode projects, refreshes
autogen-result.tar.gz when autotools are available, and builds the dependencies.

The dynamic libetpan framework links its RNP, Cyrus SASL, Json-C, zlib, and Apple
system-library dependencies. The static macOS and iOS archives do not contain
their dependencies. Applications linking a static archive must also link the
dependency libraries required by the enabled features: RNP, Cyrus SASL, Json-C,
zlib, OpenSSL Crypto as required by RNP, and the applicable Apple system
frameworks, including CFNetwork on iOS. iOS uses OpenSSL Crypto directly for
S/MIME as well. The macOS
libetpan OpenSSL transport and S/MIME backends remain disabled even though RNP's
transitive OpenSSL Crypto dependency is linked.

Apple builds use the SDK iconv by default and do not require ICU. To test the
CoreFoundation conversion backend, configure Unix macOS with --with-icu=no
--disable-iconv, or run the Unix iOS script with LIBETPAN_DISABLE_ICONV=1.
For Xcode builds, pass OTHER_CFLAGS='$(inherited) -DLIBETPAN_DISABLE_ICONV=1'.
Without iconv, small generated VISCII and KOI8-T tables provide conversions to
and from UTF-8 when CoreFoundation cannot supply those encodings.

The charset inventory tests keep decoding checks for ISO-2022-CN,
ISO-2022-CN-EXT, and ISO-2022-KR, including their recorded aliases. Encoding
checks for those five names are explicitly skipped with the Apple iconv backend:
some system versions replace their non-ASCII output with question marks.
CoreFoundation-only and ICU tests still check both directions. This test
exception does not change the library's charset support or conversion behavior.
